{
  "abstract": "Let $U_p$ be the coaxial parabola pair of papers 99 and 100 on the $n \\times n$ grid with $n = 2p$, a stock of $4n$ sites whose all-line LP relaxation is at least $1.81n$. We prove that no subset of $U_p$ with $\\lfloor 1.8n \\rfloor + 1$ points is free of collinear triples, for $p = 11, 19, 23, 31, 43, 47, 59$. Each proof is a branch-and-bound tree whose leaves are closed by a collinear triple, a counting bound, or a Lagrangian bound with exact rational multipliers. An independent checker rebuilds the line list and re-verifies every leaf in rational arithmetic. Five trees are supplied; the other three, too large for the upload limit, are regenerated deterministically by the supplied prover and checked against published SHA-256 hashes. At $p = 11$ a further tree excludes 36 points and a witness gives 35, so $\\alpha(U_{11}) = 35 = 1.59n$. Thus the LP value of $U_p$ does not transfer to the integer problem at any prime tested. As a secondary, finite observation we report solver optima for paper 47's HJSW frame with two foreign product levels offered in the central square, at $p \\le 59$; the gain over $3(p-1)$ is at most 6. Larger offers (more levels, whole-board additions, frame deletions) give caps of up to $1.59n$ points, below the known arm-core completions of the frame. Paper 32 (Corollary 2.1) already bounds fixed palettes asymptotically, so these data say nothing new about that regime; we record them as reproducible finite instances.",
